  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1203 PM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nAnother quiet day is on tap for today as a dry airmass resides over \nColorado. Latest satellite and surface observations show that mostly \nclear skies are allowing for efficient warming, with temperatures \nacross the plains already above 80 degrees, mid to upper 60s for \nthe higher elevations. Afternoon highs are expected to warm into \nthe mid to upper 80s across the plains, with a few locations \npotentially reaching 90. The higher elevations are expected to be \nabout ten degrees cooler, with mid to upper 70s to low 80s \nexpected. A line of cumulus clouds resides along a convergence \nzone stretching from the eastern Palmer Divide into the Nebraska \npanhandle where the greatest surface moisture is in place. SPC \nmesoanalysis shows MLCAPE values approaching 500 J/kg along and \neast of the boundary today. Given some noticeable vertical growth \non the latest satellite imagery, limited PoPs have been maintained\nthis afternoon, as any convective development would most likely \noccur along this boundary. \n\nNot much has changed in the short term forecast today. Friday \nis still expected to be another warm and mostly dry day across \nthe forecast area. A little increase in mid-level moisture \nstill looks to move into the higher elevations. We could see a \nfew high-based showers in our northern mountains in the \nafternoon, but instability remains limited. CAMs show potential \nfor some high-based virga showers to move across the plains in \nthe late afternoon, but this would likely just lead to some gusty \noutflows and nothing more than a trace of precipitation. Between a\nfew degrees of warming at the 700 mb level and downsloping winds,\nafternoon highs are forecast to warm into the 90s across the \nplains ahead of a cold front Friday night. A few late night \nshowers will be possible with the cold front, mainly for the \nnorthern mountains and foothills. \n\nA brief cooldown is expected on Saturday behind the cold front.\nEnsembles are in agreement that we will see some better moisture\nreturn to the forecast area throughout the day as the next \nshortwave trough moves into the Pacific Northwest and starts to \nturn flow aloft more southwesterly. However, they differ fairly \nsignificantly on how much moisture we manage to tap into. Between \nthe ECMWF and GFS ensembles, PWAT anomalies will range from 100% \nto 160% of normal Saturday afternoon. Even with the discrepancies,\nbetween the increased moisture and better instability expected (a\nfew hundred Jules of MLCAPE), scattered afternoon showers/storms \nare expected to return to the higher elevations. \n\nSome slight changes in the long term forecast are present today \nas the NBM seems to be getting a slightly better handle on the \narrival time of a few cooler air masses. Sunday is still on track \nto be a hot one, with afternoon highs expected to reach the mid \n90s across the plains. There is better agreement that moisture \nwill increase a tad more and move into the lower elevations.\nMLCAPE is expected to bump up to 600-1000 J/kg across the plains,\nwhich could allow for some stronger storms to develop.\n\nA more active and cooler pattern will remain in place through the\nweek, with multiple cold fronts expected to bring a few cooldowns\nand precipitation chances. Monday is now looking to be warmer than\npreviously expected, with afternoon temperatures within a few \ndegrees of normal ahead of a cold front that is now looking to \narrive overnight. While there is still some time for things to \nchange, it's looking like Tuesday will bring a significant \ncooldown, with afternoon highs struggling to climb out of the 60s \nfor much of the lower elevations, and at least one more cold front\npossible by the end of the week.",
